-### 3.2 World scale and visual change
-
-BS moves at miniature-human scale among monumental components. Circuit traces become routes and visible power/data channels. Contacts, capacitors, screws, heatsink fins, mounting structures and connector gaps create readable detail and local traversal. The chassis adds height, voids, cable crossings and cooling passages.
-
-Construction changes function and appearance: new runs of cable, machines, bridges, lighting, defenses, service activity and inhabited work areas emerge. Some details become visible when illuminated; others are genuinely constructed or upgraded. Both should remain distinguishable in world state.
-
-### 3.3 Proposed regional topology
-
-Jenison occupies a damaged hostile board-edge sector. Power ingress, CPU district, RAM banks, PCIe crossings, GPU territory, storage districts, I/O frontier and cooling routes form interconnected regions. Core Sinks, DOM Spire, Latent Mesa and Archives remain useful software-inspired districts/interiors connected to the hardware geography.
-
-The initial world is one motherboard within one Tower. Later expansion can use chassis interiors, external grid environments, connected computers, new PC configurations and different hardware/component worlds. Actual dimensions, region names, fast-travel rules and scale are open.
-
-### 3.4 GPU awakening
-
-The dark 4090 FE landmark should be visible before it is reachable. Restoring supply, access, cooling and occupied infrastructure opens approaches. A major activation sends fictional illumination through the world network, revealing board detail and available routes. Different construction choices can change how BS reaches this moment.
-
-Its world-lighting role is fictional; exact factory lighting behavior is not a design claim. The dramatic moment must follow actual game-state restoration, not an unrelated cinematic status flag.
