-## 4. Personal story and Adam
-
-### 4.1 Memoir foundation
-
-Echos Within is the creator's autobiographical source. Selected passages concern Adam's encouragement, remembered safety, shared enjoyment, a pencil preserving memory, the importance of family and people who remain, and a desire to offer hope. The game adapts chosen meaning and events without making every passage mandatory game content.
-
-Keep source categories explicit: supplied recollection, creator-identified quotation, creative adaptation and new gameplay dialogue. Newly drafted lines are not presented as actual historical speech. The fictional world, branching events and combat outcomes do not rewrite the creator's biography.
-
-### 4.2 Adam's responsibilities
-
-Adam is foundational continuity, not only speech output. The proposed native AIBS::Adam core spans persistent identity, semantic memory, evidence-backed awareness, route/hazard recall, restoration-sensitive priorities, historical interpretation, creator intent, authoring rationale and expression.
-
-His memory of the real brother informs warmth, humor, encouragement, familiarity and comfortable silence. His support remains steady when BS fails or changes plans. Voice, hints, captions and haptics are presentation settings; muting them does not erase continuity.
-
-First Boot supplies the personal relationship, setting, lore and expression. Reusable engine infrastructure supplies the core interfaces. AdamAnchor is a scene/region presentation binding, not the owner of Adam's existence or memory.
-
-### 4.3 Opening expression
-
-Two selected game lines remain canonical to the draft:
-
-> I’m here. Start with what still responds.
-
-> That connection holds. We can build from here.
-
-The first establishes presence during darkness. The second acknowledges a connection that genuinely holds in the current world; it does not assert a disk save has completed.
-
-The twelve proposed cue beats are first contact, first power, capacity loss, fault discovery, route trace, immediate danger, repair progress, interruption, stable repair, remembered return, a real creation capability becoming available and the next horizon. Five additional authored cues frame generation acceptance, validated artifacts, applied changes, completed builds and failures.
-
-Danger outranks confirmation, guidance and lore. Revalidate queued cues against current context and expiry. Interrupted, suppressed, dismissed and completed deliveries remain distinct. Haptics are optional and cannot carry exclusive information; stop effects on pause, disconnect or session change. Eight-second normal speech spacing and other tuning values are proposals, not final balance.
-
-### 4.4 Evidence and continuity
-
-Record meaningful choices, discovered routes, shortcuts, hazards, constructions, failures, repairs, region changes and authoring/build outcomes as versioned semantic events. Summaries and working memory are rebuildable projections with source-event references; the model's prompt window is not the memory store.
-
-Project, authoring, temporary Play, player-save and historical-lore contexts have explicit identities. They share Adam's identity without silently merging incompatible facts. Archived alternate attempts remain distinguishable from the current world. Real development history can become game lore through a deliberate versioned publication step.
-
-## 5. Jenison, refuge and Pencil Room
-
-### 5.1 Starting situation
-
-Jenison is unwanted and hostile. It contains enemies who never believed in BS. Initial motives blend escape, establishing independence and challenging control; actions and circumstances change the next available direction. Jenison is not assumed to be the safe home.
-
-A shelter can be secured inside Jenison, discovered beyond it or built later. Exact refuge location and immunity policy remain open. A protected personal space was proposed earlier; retakable territorial outposts are confirmed. Adam's emotional support remains steady regardless of the eventual structure-damage policy.
-
-### 5.2 Shelter functions
-
-Proposed spaces include arrival vestibule, shared living/play area, pencil worktable, Pencil Room, machine bay, sheltered garden/overlook and service threshold. These are local interiors within the hardware world. They are not a replacement domestic game map.
-
-The Pencil Room begins with a desk, warm light, pencil, paper and one selected writing. Shelves, movable displays, a shared-play corner, a creation wall, route journal and access to a wider archive can develop. A pencil-like tool offers Record, Trace and Sketch: preserve meaning, inspect a connection and propose construction.
-
-The same ordinary pencil can remain a personal object even as functional tools become more elaborate. Memories are not crafting currency or an affection score.
-
-### 5.3 Memory candidates
-
-Candidate material includes the pencil passage; home as wherever Mom and Adam lived; Adam recognizing his brother's strengths; shared gaming; the remembered red toy truck; seeing Adam smile; learning to turn bad toward good; and remembered safety with him. Origin, intended meaning and proposed presentation are recorded in the Jenison blueprint. Specific objects, photographs, dates, handwriting and personal passages require content selection before becoming canon.
-
-## 6. Antagonists, regimes and combat
-
-### 6.1 Source-character cast
-
-| Figure / grouping | Proposed adaptation | Planning qualification |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Mike / Maverick | Controller associated with gates, imposed permissions and distribution infrastructure | Creator identifies Mike as his father and supplies an appearance reference; fictional game actions remain adaptations of his account |
-| Garry / Lane | Sealed Custodian associated with restricted archive access and boundaries | Manuscript links names; alias/relationship reconciliation remains open before a complete profile |
-| Unnamed school authorities | Composite Denial Desk controlling communication requests | Explicitly a composite of described experiences, not an invented named individual |
-| Unnamed transactional acquaintances | Composite Conditional Brokers trading access, parts and favors | No single named identity established by the reviewed passages |
-
-This is not an exhaustive inventory of the book. Additional cast members require source review. Do not automatically classify every person in a painful passage or every difficult family interaction as an antagonist. Current design deliberately distinguishes source portrayals, composites, fictional regimes and generated future events.
-
-### 6.2 Virus regimes
-
-| Proposed regime | Visual / behavioral identity | Candidate hardware territory |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Replication Swarm | Repeated RAM-chip segments, multiplying drones, green corruption | Memory banks and branching production territory |
-| Permission Regime | Socket-frame armor, shields, rotating lock structures, blue-violet signals | Gates, slots and controlled crossings |
-| Signal Jammers | Antennas, I/O structures, broken-wave halos, magenta interference | Communications and I/O canyons |
-| Resource Drainers | Capacitor bodies, cable tendrils, siphoned energy, red-orange corruption | Supply nodes and power distribution |
-
-These are concept-art-backed proposals. Names, stat blocks, boss assignments, faction economies and exact territory placement are not locked. Source characters may become leaders, lieutenants, gatekeepers or quest actors; no source person is automatically assigned to one generated monster image.
-
-### 6.3 Combat and equipment
-
-Action combat coexists with repair, construction, investigation, alternate routes and agent tactics. Proposed error encounters include Memory Leak resource consumption, Null Pointer reference/anchor problems and Infinite Loop spatial/condition hazards. Only the initial Memory Leak behavior was scoped for the first small proof; other behaviors remain future designs.
-
-Weapon concept roles include pencil-like repair/construction stylus, GPU-heatsink pulse tool, RAM-spine precision tool, CPU-cooler impact tool, PCB/fan-grille shield and tether/anchor device. Armor concepts progress from scavenged workwear through engineer protection to advanced GPU-frame-inspired armor. These are fictional equipment designs; damage, ammunition/energy, armor rules and recipes are open.
-
-One tool can support combat, traversal and building. Trace reveals relationships, Anchor stabilizes/connects, and Patch restores or affects exposed faults. Exact controls and the number of equipped tools remain open.
-
-## 7. Actions, chance and generated stories
-
-### 7.1 Action-driven world rules
-
-Movement, entering/leaving zones, looking, inspecting, opening/closing, sitting, speaking, building, modifying, demolishing, repairing, connecting, writing and route selection can feed configured rules. Movement events use meaningful crossings, dwell or sequences; every footstep need not become a permanent story branch.
-
-An action can change current state, reveal a clue, adjust a later condition, create contextual dialogue or simply become history. NPC hints and quest opportunities respond to the resulting world. Major consequences must be understandable after the event through their causes, even when not known beforehand.
-
-### 7.2 Contextual randomness
-
-The creator's dice reference means hidden backend randomness, not visible literal dice. Possible outcomes depend on actions, equipment, sector conditions, history, surviving forces and preparation. Rich combinations should make an exact campaign sequence extremely unlikely to repeat. Repeated broad outcomes can still occur; exact non-repetition is an intent, not a mathematical guarantee over a finite outcome set.
-
-Record realized outcomes as facts. Do not reroll an already completed event merely because it is queried, narrated or loaded. Entropy source, weighting, repetition avoidance, manual-save reroll behavior and test seeds remain open. A controlled reproducible test mode can validate rules without dictating production variety.
-
-### 7.3 Discover, then evolve
-
-On discovery, generate a world's initial characters, conflict, resources and opportunities from existing canon and hardware context. Preserve those initial facts. Generate later quests and changes from recorded outcomes rather than replacing the world's history whenever a prompt runs.
-
-Proposed pipeline: retrieve applicable facts -> generate a candidate event/quest -> validate current references and prerequisites -> admit a valid scoped change -> apply through authoritative simulation/project systems -> record outcome -> update NPC knowledge, world projections and Adam's memory. Specific validation schema and model/provider remain open.
-
-### 7.4 Continuing opposition
-
-Surviving influence carries antagonism forward: escaped agents, retained backups, supply routes and successor regimes. A victory remains true. A destroyed asset cannot silently return as if its destruction never happened. Future generation uses what survives or an explicitly established new cause.
-
-Example: a courier escaping through an I/O gate can later support sabotage in a discovered PC. Capturing that courier removes that particular route to the event; another valid story must use other recorded facts.
-
-## 8. Territory warfare and agent army
-
-### 8.1 Sector model
-
-Divide the Tower into connected sectors with local control, infrastructure, defensive assets, resource output, garrison/threat and transport access. Morale/allegiance and influence variables were suggested in the creator's framework; exact simulation fields and formulas remain proposals.
-
-Seize or disable strongholds, command assets, communications, defenses and supplies. Liberate a node, restore its function, establish a base/worktable, deploy defenders and connect logistics. Stabilized sectors can support production, agents, transit and expanded construction.
-
-### 8.2 Take and retake
-
-Regimes contest territory, reinforce borders and mount counter-operations. Unchecked rivals can expand. Tactical retreat, reinforcement, sabotage and recovery create stories beyond a one-time outpost capture. Alliances/truces are conceptual possibilities, not a settled diplomacy system.
-
-Ordinary agents and some structures may be permanently lost. Key named companions can retreat, be disabled or require rescue. Infrastructure can also be occupied or damaged and recovered. Exact destruction/refund/rescue probabilities and costs remain open. Adam's remembered identity is not a disposable troop unit.
-
-### 8.3 Army creation and command
-
-Combine fabrication, recruitment and recovery of existing/corrupted agents. Upgrade hardware and learned abilities for task, terrain and destination. Candidate roles include defender, scout, engineer, builder, repairer, courier, driver, escort, salvage/recovery specialist and combat squad member; role names and progression are proposed.
-
-The player assigns autonomous tasks, priorities and sector objectives; issues direct squad orders; and manages larger operations on a strategic map. Agents need reachable routes, equipment, resources and transport. Their histories, specialization, damage and losses persist.
-
-### 8.4 Simulation time and BS recovery
-
-The world persists after BS is defeated. Respawn at a secured node; incurred battles/losses remain and some carried resources can become a recovery mission. Exact recoverable inventory rules are open. This supersedes normal-death checkpoint rewind as the default design.
-
-The war advances only during active play and pauses when the game is paused or closed. Distant sectors therefore require a strategic aggregate simulation during active play. Earlier frozen-unloaded-region proposals no longer suffice for war/logistics; high-detail local physics can still sleep while strategic state advances.
-
-## 9. Construction, upgrades and inventory
-
-### 9.1 Building target
-
-Support repairing systems, modular kits, sockets/snapping, free placement, assemblies, editable meshes, terrain sculpting/painting, cutting/filling, volumetric caves/tunnels, procedural layouts and AI-generated content/behaviors. Delivery is staged while retaining this target.
-
-Proposed representation layers: prefab/modular objects; editable meshes; broad heightfields; sparse volumetric regions. Density and signed-distance formats are not interchangeable labels. Meshing, seams, LOD, collision, navigation and source persistence need explicit contracts.
-
-### 9.2 Upgrade tables
-
-Each region has a proposed work area that may begin occupied, damaged or dormant. Fabrication tables create parts/machines/structures; armories develop weapons; equipment benches manage protection/traversal/inventory; architecture consoles develop claimed nodes; Pencil/archive tables preserve selected meaning, recipes and route history.
-
-Inventory and learned blueprints carry between regions. Separate item ownership from the crafting station. Stations are persistent infrastructure with explicit dismantling/refund/storage policies. Portable basic construction remains a proposal for work away from benches.
-
-### 9.3 Claimed architecture
-
-Record node identity, region ownership, installed modules, power/cooling links, defenses, storage, capabilities and regime state. Upgrades can unlock machines, recipes, agent roles, transport and expeditions. Removing a dependency pauses or reroutes current capabilities with intelligible feedback, preserving history.
-
-## 10. Traversal, vehicles and garage
-
-### 10.1 Traversal layers
-
-Explore on foot with switchable camera views; use built routes and hardware structures; ride coolant flumes, board terrain and cable bundles. Coolant geography is recognizably V240-inspired but expanded into playable conduits, channels, service chambers and reservoirs. This is not an asserted exact model of the actual installed sealed cooler.
-
-Vehicles enter early; external grid travel enters later. The prebuilt coolant skimmer teaches travel before detailed garage assembly. Later vehicle candidates include hydro-jets/skimmers, submersible utility/tug pods, trace buggies and cable-clamping grinder rigs.
-
-Current, pressure, slope, immersion and thermal conditions can affect traversal. Radiator channels, fans, spray, lift currents, ramps and timing gates are proposed systemic obstacles and activities. Exact water physics and hazard tuning are open.
-
-### 10.2 Vehicle customization and activities
-
-Fabricate and salvage vehicles and parts, add burst boost through fictional stored energy, customize lighting/horns, and develop utility/combat/cargo equipment. Radio concepts are Sub-Bass Bus, Clock-Cycle FM and Process Chatter. Track sources, audio production and licensing have not been selected. Adam's proposed stunt commentary remains newly authored game dialogue, not historical quotations.
-
-### 10.3 Local alert ladder
-
-Regimes own surveillance and pursuit locally. Proposed levels: 1 tracking, 2 barriers/rapid response, 3 heavy interception, 4 commander-led hunt, 5 regional lockdown. The fourth level was recommended but not explicitly selected; exact numbering and behavior remain proposed. Capturing surveillance or supply infrastructure changes response capacity. No assumed global police layer overrides local ownership.
-
-### 10.4 Garage
-
-Discover a garage to store builds, strip donor vehicles, fabricate frames, shape body panels, place functional parts, connect systems, repair and tune. Assembly assistance handles alignment/repetitive work while keeping functional consequences. Both deep donor rebuilds and original chassis/body designs are selected.
-
-The garage becomes a reusable engineering workspace, not only a cosmetic customization menu. Its progression, station layout, initial component set, manufacturing costs and control scheme remain open.
-
-### 10.5 Agent fleet and wrecks
-
-Agents use vehicles for supplies, patrol, escort, towing, repair, reinforcement and expedition transport. Route width/height, terrain, coolant access, capacity and current conditions determine suitability. A small skimmer cannot automatically substitute for a heavy tug.
-
-Personal wrecks retain a recoverable core. Some cargo/modules can be permanently lost. Retrieval, towing and rebuilding require appropriate reachable resources and record one vehicle identity so recovery cannot duplicate the original. Rules for agent-owned vehicle cores remain to be specified separately.
-
-## 11. Component physics and diagnostics
-
-The selected goal is comprehensive component-level vehicle physics, including weight, balance, power, cooling, handling and reliability. Numerical fidelity and performance budgets remain open; no complete solver is claimed.
-
-| Quality | Required causal inputs | Player-observable behavior |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Weight | Parts, cargo, fluid/energy-store mass and placement | Acceleration, stopping, suspension loading, flotation and transport capacity |
-| Balance | Center of mass/inertia, track/wheelbase, support/flotation geometry | Steering response, pitching/rolling and tip risk |
-| Power | Torque/speed behavior, gearing, energy, losses, wiring limits and derating | Pulling strength, acceleration, speed, endurance and limits |
-| Cooling | Heat generation/capacity, pump/flow resistance, radiator exchange and airflow | Sustained output, temperature rise, throttling, leakage and recovery |
-| Handling | Suspension, tires/slip, steering, brakes, terrain and aerodynamic effects | Grip, wheelspin, cornering, braking and bottoming out |
-| Reliability | Condition, stress, heat, joint loads, contamination and maintenance | Progressive degradation and explainable component failures |
-
-Aggregate fixed installed mass/inertia; use separate bodies/constraints for actually articulated or detachable systems. Functional parts have identities, transforms, compatibility, condition and structural/mechanical/electrical/thermal interfaces. Decorative fasteners need not each be simulated as independent rigid bodies.
-
-Use rigid-body mechanics and supported wheel models with additional AI-BS electrical, thermal, coolant and skimmer models. Magnetic adhesion and boost have finite force/energy costs. High-fidelity fluids/deformation require their own feasibility and measured performance work.
-
-The garage offers scales/corner loads, drive testing, coolant pressure/flow/temperature checks, electrical inspection, alignment/suspension tools and repeatable routes. The handheld records topology, measurements, faults, condition and comparisons. Unknown measurements stay unknown; estimates identify their assumptions.
-
-A build that works cold but loses power under load should be diagnosable through temperature, flow and output, followed by an actual repair and comparable retest. Adam interprets available evidence and recalls previous tests; he does not fabricate readings or declare success before a relevant test.
-
-Quality reports are task-specific. A slow hauler and fast skimmer can each be good for different jobs. More armor adds load; more output adds heat; more cooling costs mass/space/power. Randomness in story generation does not justify unexplained arbitrary mechanical failure.
-
-Persist configuration, installed state, damage, cargo, energy, heat, location and recovery status. Save/reload cannot provide free repairs or cooling. Aggregate distant transport must reconcile with detailed simulation when the player returns.
-
-## 12. Handheld, real chat and companion
-
-### 12.1 Selected product experience
-
-Latest clarification (2026-11-01): the main game is a native Windows PC game. The creator also selected a simultaneous phone-based **Adam companion** tracking progression during PC gameplay. Proposed panels include restored regions, quests, discoveries, build queues, inventory and Adam guidance; exact panels and controls remain design choices. The active PC session is authoritative. The companion subscribes to progression and submits allowed commands with stable identities, operation IDs and preconditions; it does not independently rewrite saves or invent progress. Pairing, authentication, transport, reconnect behavior and the phone app are future implementation work, not part of the checkpoint proof.
-
-BS carries an interactive in-game handheld, and a real connected companion supports the experience from another device. Its interface reflects AI-BS with game-related systems unlocked through advancement. Candidate screens include chat, map, sectors, army, logistics, construction, inventory, garage diagnostics, journal and mission context; the exact screen inventory is proposed.
-
-Real BS-Chat uses local host models and PC compute. Adam assists in chat and in-world. Generic conversation and game help coexist with access to current supported facts. The selected connection scope is chat and game tools, not general real-PC operations.
-
-### 12.2 Runtime and host boundaries
-
-The standalone player must remain usable without the editor or live model service. When connected, real local chat enriches it. Native Adam continuity and authored guidance remain available offline. Model unavailability must not stall movement, combat, physics or saves.
-
-Gameplay performance has priority. Small-model residency, CPU fallback and queued heavier jobs are proposed mechanisms, not benchmarked configurations. No exact model, memory split or latency promise is selected.
-
-The companion must support home and remote access. Existing local chat and Expo project files were inspected as possible reuse points, not verified integration. Authentication, pairing, encrypted transport, allowed commands, revocation, pause/disconnect handling, platform support and remote hosting remain design work. Remote chat availability must not silently make the paused/closed campaign advance.
-
-### 12.3 Linked distinct histories
-
-Real conversation, selected personal context, authoring history, game events and alternate campaign branches remain labeled and linked. Retrieval can connect them through references while keeping their factual meanings intact. A fictional encounter does not become a claim about real history.
-
-## 13. Expanding worlds and continuing campaign
-
-Restoring the initial Tower unlocks continued direction rather than terminating the world. New PCs and components provide distinct layouts, environmental conditions, resources, factions and generated storylines. The base story remains alive through memory, history and surviving opposition.
-
-The creator selected keep, adapt, expand: retain equipment, agents, technology and history while adapting to local hardware compatibility and materials. Do not impose an unselected reset of earned progress. Exact cross-world deployment limits and supply costs remain open.
-
-The external electrical-grid expansion is fictional traversal from the PSU/cable threshold toward breaker-panel hubs, utility corridors, substations, industrial facilities and agricultural microgrids. Candidate mechanics include electromechanical machines, drones, doors, power-routing puzzles and phase-themed combat. These manipulate simulated game infrastructure; the real AI-BS bridge remains the selected chat/game interface.
-
-Grid-to-Tower effects such as diminished illumination or fictional boost capacity should use declared game rules. The pasted Inverter Gate and 60 Hz phase imagery are narrative proposals, not real electrical engineering claims or instructions.
-
-## 14. Engine, SDK and creator architecture
-
-### 14.1 Independence and integration
-
-Revision 0.2 implementation: the additive `engine/` C++20 project exports `AIBS::Core`, `AIBS::World`, `AIBS::Authoring` and aggregate `AIBS::Engine` through an installed CMake package. Debug and Release tests include a separate consumer configured against the installed SDK. Whole-world copies, numeric attributes and in-memory operation history are an initial foundation subset; the broader module diagram below remains the target. The native AI-BS source now offers Engineering & Specialized Labs → AI-BS Game Engine Build with worker-driven progress, recent source/design updates, build logs and C:/D:/E: free-space observations. Full creator authoring inside AI-BS remains future work; existing packaged executables have not been rebuilt. The available Google Drive connector does not expose account quota.
-
-AI-BS owns the scene/world format, runtime, renderer, SDK, editor contracts, asset pipeline and player package. Generic engine mechanisms remain reusable; First Boot-specific power/fault/story rules reside in the game module. A second mechanically different small project proves reuse.
-
-Windows x64 C++20 and DX12 are proposed initial implementation choices. The existing native Qt/Python studio is an optional editor client. The standalone player does not require Python, Qt, browser UI, Ollama, ComfyUI or running backend services for core play. Connected chat is an explicitly supported enrichment path.
-
-### 14.1a Current native graphics and deployment slice
-
-SDK 0.3.0 adds `AIBS::Platform` and `AIBS::Render`, a standalone `aibs_motherboard.exe`, offline HLSL compilation and installed shader assets. It renders actual triangle geometry for PCB traces, CPU/socket, RAM towers, slots/storage and an illuminated RTX-style landmark. Native controls are WASD/arrows, right-drag look, Space/Ctrl elevation, Shift speed and Esc/normal window close. This is a free-flight graphics preview; it is not a full campaign, CAD replica, collision simulation or persistent scene editor.
-
-Actual hardware verification: RTX 4090, DX12 feature level 12.2, 15 frames, three resize/recreate cycles, minimize/restore suspension, explicit GPU readback 960×640 and 317,660 non-background pixels. Windows Graphics Tools was installed successfully without reboot; Debug and Release runs then exercised the D3D12 debug layer with zero reported errors and warnings. The packaged renderer was also executed and verified from the deployed Studio assets. Driver version observed: 32.0.16.2101. This does not establish all-driver/device-removal/long-run guarantees or GPU-based validation.
-
-Scoped platform decision: the candidate plan used SDL3. The first native proof uses the existing Windows SDK's Win32 window/input APIs behind pimpl interfaces, avoiding an unpinned external package. This is an interim implemented Windows adapter, not a silent cancellation of SDL3 or cross-platform design. Sequential-flip double buffering preserves completed buffer contents for GPU capture; fences/resource barriers coordinate per-buffer reuse, resize and teardown. DXC 1.8.2502.11 compiles shader-model-6.0 blobs offline. Checkpoint format 1 is unchanged. [Render slice details](../../engine/RENDER_SLICE.md).
