-## 15. Data, saves and continuity
-
-### 15.0 Implemented local checkpoint slice (SDK 0.2.0)
-
-`AIBS::Persistence` now provides format-1 `.aibs` checkpoints in an additive standalone Windows adapter. Worlds and entities receive OS-random UUID v4 identities; authored aliases remain convenience lookups. Retired UUIDs are preserved and cannot be recycled. Adam events include scoped world identity, realized UUID-bearing edits and supplied descriptions. A reused alias cannot redirect old memory.
-
-A checkpoint stores the numeric schema, world, retired identities, complete realized edit journal, original accepted requests/receipts and undo/redo together. Load validates the entire bounded file, journal replay, schemas, scopes and history before publishing state. Saving an existing slot checks its world and previous head; stale sessions cannot overwrite newer progress. Explicit save success is separate from an accepted in-memory edit. Play copies do not automatically write to an authored save.
-
-The Windows writer uses a same-directory temporary file, checked write/flush, exclusive cooperation lock and replacement publication. Separate process tests cover restart and forced exits immediately before/after publication. Invalid loads preserve live world, memory and checkpoint head. Tests also reject corrupt/truncated/future-format/extra-byte data, checksummed invalid fields/scopes/counts and invalid transient creations. CRC32 detects accidental corruption, not tampering. Hardware power-loss immunity and network-share guarantees are not established.
-
-This is an explicitly scoped interim deviation from the proposed SQLite LocalSaveStore/MasterDbSaveStore implementation: it supplies a working native checkpoint proof without introducing a third-party database dependency or changing existing AI-BS databases. SQLite adapters, migrations, large streamed saves, async workers, auto-save, alternate-timeline archival and generation-checked runtime handles remain later work. The current synchronous single-owner adapter and whole-world copies are limited to small scenes. Budgets are 16 MiB/file, 4,096-byte strings, 10,000 items/collection, 200,000 entity/field expansion units and replay work units plus a 64 MiB estimated replay-copy budget. Failed saves retain the last published checkpoint and report failure. Interrupted temporary files are ignored on load and counted in generated storage; automatic orphan cleanup is future work.
-
-Requirement: durable world/Adam continuity and SDK checkpoint API. Original design: SQLite-backed local/master stores. Interim choice: bounded binary local adapter with a separate `AIBS::Persistence` CMake target. Justification: exercise the world/continuity/save boundary now using only the native toolchain. Compatibility: SDK 0.2 rebuild required; no pre-0.2 native checkpoint format existed; future formats/schemas reject until an explicit migration exists. Status: implemented and verified for this local slice, not a replacement of the broader approved persistence target. Details: [SAVE_FORMAT.md](../../engine/SAVE_FORMAT.md), [README](../../engine/README.md), [build evidence](../../engine/BUILD_STATUS.md).
-
-### 15.1 Foundational types
-
-Persistent entity/asset/project UUIDs survive reload; transient runtime handles carry generations. Components use stable type/field IDs, explicit versions, typed defaults/requirements, constraints, inspector metadata and separate scene/save/runtime/derived policies. Units are explicit; the proposed world convention is meters, seconds, radians, normalized xyzw quaternions, left-handed +Y-up/+Z-forward coordinates and linear colors.
-
-The starter design registry has sixteen component types and 82 fields: Identity, Transform, MeshRenderer, Camera, Light, Collider, RigidBody, CharacterController, AudioEmitter, Interactable, PowerNode, DevicePresentation, Fault, Objective, WorldGate and AdamAnchor. This is a planning registry, not implemented reflection. Army, vehicles, strategic sectors, dialogue, generated stories and companion systems require further types.
-
-### 15.2 State categories
-
-Keep authored sources, compiled/cooked assets, caches, active simulation, player saves, authoring history and personal source material distinguishable. Prefabs map prototype-local IDs to instance UUIDs. Variants, instance overrides and player-save overrides have explicit inheritance and migration rules.
-
-Proposed new records include SectorState, AgentInstance, TaskAssignment, VehicleBlueprint/Instance, PartInstance, Route/LogisticsOrder, RegimeState, QuestDefinition/Instance, NarrativeFact/Event and CompanionSession. Names and field layouts are not locked APIs.
-
-### 15.3 Operations and coherent publication
-
-An operation identifies version, actor/context, target IDs/bounds, expected revision, preconditions, parameters and inverse/delta information. Accept, apply and durably commit are distinct states. Persist a durable operation result with its delta so retries cannot duplicate entities, charges or history.
-
-Render/collision artifacts prepare asynchronously and publish matching ready revisions at safe boundaries. Navigation can follow with explicit dirty/unavailable state until rebuilt. Stale jobs cannot overwrite newer edits. Scenes, terrain source, created entities and deletion tombstones retain stable ownership across unloaded regions.
-
-### 15.4 Storage and time
-
-MasterDbSaveStore uses new engine_game_* tables in the existing master database for integrated mode. LocalSaveStore uses the same game schema in per-user storage for portable standalone play. Export/import is versioned and includes selected game state/content references, not unrelated database contents.
-
-Store large terrain or generated source content outside checkpoint blobs with durable references. Save snapshots and corresponding Adam event heads stay consistent. Game systems do not synchronously query/write databases in the frame loop. Failure preserves the last committed checkpoint and reports current durability honestly.
-
-Ordinary BS defeat uses persistent respawn, not timeline rewind. Deliberately loading an older save still needs a branch policy, archived-future handling and explicit active-history projection. Story generation cannot quietly merge mutually exclusive pasts.
-
-### 15.5 Proposed timing and budgets
-
-The earlier runtime proposal starts at 60 Hz fixed simulation, bounded catch-up and immutable render snapshots. Vehicle substeps and strategic update cadence require profiling. Fixed stepping alone does not promise cross-platform bitwise physics equivalence.
-
-Initial two-room targets were 1080p with p95 frame time <=16.7 ms and p99 <=33.3 ms, player working set <=4 GiB and a renderer soft VRAM cap of 8 GiB further constrained by actual adapter budget. These are provisional slice targets, not measurements or approved full-world budgets. Army size, vehicle counts, regions and simultaneous inference change the workload and require new benchmarks.
-
-Existing telemetry code contains static fallback values; those cannot authorize actual shared GPU use. Use measured availability and valid-status reporting. Game rendering has priority over heavier model jobs; the exact allocation policy is still open.
