-## 19. Delivery stages and acceptance
-
-### 19.1 Existing engineering sequence
-
-M0 toolchain/build skeleton; M1 native rendering/input/lifecycle; M2 identity/components/assets/Adam contracts; M3 playable repair/saves/native continuity; M4 native authoring/editor; M5 AI generation enrichment; M6 chapter/package; M7 streamed worlds and scalable detail. These remain proposed dependencies, not completed milestones.
-
-R0 foundation, R1 playable, R2 hybrid creator alpha, R3 editable streamed worlds and R4 general engine beta describe product capabilities. The enlarged warfare/vehicle/companion/ongoing-story vision exceeds the original two-room milestone plan and needs separate scheduling; it is not silently absorbed into one M7 task.
-
-### 19.2 Proposed expanded workstreams
-
-| Track | Deliverable | Evidence required |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Foundation | Native player, authored/cooked scene, stable lifecycle | Real native rendering and packaged launch, headless contract checks |
-| Personal continuity | Adam identity/events, authored opening, source provenance | Offline recall, correct contextual cues and branch isolation |
-| Construction | Editable structures, first claim, inventory, worktable | Build/dismantle/restore and save/reload without duplication or stale unlocks |
-| Territory/army | Sector contest, roles, logistics and hybrid orders | Retake/defend/rescue scenarios with persistent losses and active-play strategic updates |
-| Narrative generation | Discover/evolve worlds and fact-grounded quests | No invented current facts, contradictory returns or invalidated completed outcomes |
-| Traversal | Starter skimmer, coolant route and local pursuit | Controllable travel, physical feedback, recovery and region consequences |
-| Garage/physics | Donor/custom builds and diagnostic loop | Causal mass/thermal/handling changes and comparable retests |
-| Handheld/companion | In-world device, remote companion, local chat/game tools | Authenticated sessions, correct save context, disconnect behavior and frame-budget evidence |
-| World expansion | Additional PCs, hardware adaptation and later grid | Carried history/equipment, local compatibility and continuing goals |
-
-### 19.3 Cross-system acceptance scenarios
-
-1. Start in Jenison, obtain a tool/skimmer, choose a path, establish a claim and save/reopen the changed world.
-2. Build a bridge: player crossing works; NPC/fleet routing updates after ready navigation; removal invalidates current access while preserving historical crossings.
-3. Open/close doors and build/dismantle machines: dependent quests and hints remain coherent, with alternate progression.
-4. Liberate, reinforce and lose a sector: production, routes, agents, structures and recovery reflect actual events rather than a reset capture flag.
-5. Defeat BS: respawn while world losses persist; recover eligible carried resources without duplication.
-6. Generate a new world's story from established canon; resolve an event; reload and retain its actual outcome.
-7. Destroy a backup/capture an escaped agent: subsequent generation respects that lost source of recurring influence.
-8. Build a vehicle, overload/rebalance/reconfigure cooling, retest, wreck and recover it; retain configuration, damage and cargo correctly.
-9. Pause/close the game: war time stops; real chat does not silently advance it.
-10. Connect a companion remotely, use game tools, change session/save, disconnect: reject stale commands and preserve context boundaries.
-11. Delay/disable inference: movement, combat, native Adam continuity, authored guidance and saves remain usable.
-12. Save fails: preserve the last committed checkpoint and report failure; do not claim applied changes reached disk.
-13. Create and package a second small game through AI, visuals and code without adding its story rules to generic engine source.
-
-All are future acceptance requirements. Documentation checks or concept-image inspection do not satisfy them.
