+## 7. Actions, chance and generated stories
+
+### 7.1 Action-driven world rules
+
+Movement, entering/leaving zones, looking, inspecting, opening/closing, sitting, speaking, building, modifying, demolishing, repairing, connecting, writing and route selection can feed configured rules. Movement events use meaningful crossings, dwell or sequences; every footstep need not become a permanent story branch.
+
+An action can change current state, reveal a clue, adjust a later condition, create contextual dialogue or simply become history. NPC hints and quest opportunities respond to the resulting world. Major consequences must be understandable after the event through their causes, even when not known beforehand.
+
+### 7.2 Contextual randomness
+
+The creator's dice reference means hidden backend randomness, not visible literal dice. Possible outcomes depend on actions, equipment, sector conditions, history, surviving forces and preparation. Rich combinations should make an exact campaign sequence extremely unlikely to repeat. Repeated broad outcomes can still occur; exact non-repetition is an intent, not a mathematical guarantee over a finite outcome set.
+
+Record realized outcomes as facts. Do not reroll an already completed event merely because it is queried, narrated or loaded. Entropy source, weighting, repetition avoidance, manual-save reroll behavior and test seeds remain open. A controlled reproducible test mode can validate rules without dictating production variety.
+
+### 7.3 Discover, then evolve
+
+On discovery, generate a world's initial characters, conflict, resources and opportunities from existing canon and hardware context. Preserve those initial facts. Generate later quests and changes from recorded outcomes rather than replacing the world's history whenever a prompt runs.
+
+Proposed pipeline: retrieve applicable facts -> generate a candidate event/quest -> validate current references and prerequisites -> admit a valid scoped change -> apply through authoritative simulation/project systems -> record outcome -> update NPC knowledge, world projections and Adam's memory. Specific validation schema and model/provider remain open.
+
+### 7.4 Continuing opposition
+
+Surviving influence carries antagonism forward: escaped agents, retained backups, supply routes and successor regimes. A victory remains true. A destroyed asset cannot silently return as if its destruction never happened. Future generation uses what survives or an explicitly established new cause.
+
+Example: a courier escaping through an I/O gate can later support sabotage in a discovered PC. Capturing that courier removes that particular route to the event; another valid story must use other recorded facts.
+
+## 8. Territory warfare and agent army
+
+### 8.1 Sector model
+
+Divide the Tower into connected sectors with local control, infrastructure, defensive assets, resource output, garrison/threat and transport access. Morale/allegiance and influence variables were suggested in the creator's framework; exact simulation fields and formulas remain proposals.
+
+Seize or disable strongholds, command assets, communications, defenses and supplies. Liberate a node, restore its function, establish a base/worktable, deploy defenders and connect logistics. Stabilized sectors can support production, agents, transit and expanded construction.
+
+### 8.2 Take and retake
+
+Regimes contest territory, reinforce borders and mount counter-operations. Unchecked rivals can expand. Tactical retreat, reinforcement, sabotage and recovery create stories beyond a one-time outpost capture. Alliances/truces are conceptual possibilities, not a settled diplomacy system.
+
+Ordinary agents and some structures may be permanently lost. Key named companions can retreat, be disabled or require rescue. Infrastructure can also be occupied or damaged and recovered. Exact destruction/refund/rescue probabilities and costs remain open. Adam's remembered identity is not a disposable troop unit.
+
+### 8.3 Army creation and command
+
+Combine fabrication, recruitment and recovery of existing/corrupted agents. Upgrade hardware and learned abilities for task, terrain and destination. Candidate roles include defender, scout, engineer, builder, repairer, courier, driver, escort, salvage/recovery specialist and combat squad member; role names and progression are proposed.
+
+The player assigns autonomous tasks, priorities and sector objectives; issues direct squad orders; and manages larger operations on a strategic map. Agents need reachable routes, equipment, resources and transport. Their histories, specialization, damage and losses persist.
+
+### 8.4 Simulation time and BS recovery
+
+The world persists after BS is defeated. Respawn at a secured node; incurred battles/losses remain and some carried resources can become a recovery mission. Exact recoverable inventory rules are open. This supersedes normal-death checkpoint rewind as the default design.
+
+The war advances only during active play and pauses when the game is paused or closed. Distant sectors therefore require a strategic aggregate simulation during active play. Earlier frozen-unloaded-region proposals no longer suffice for war/logistics; high-detail local physics can still sleep while strategic state advances.
