+### 14.1 Independence and integration
+
+Revision 0.2 implementation: the additive `engine/` C++20 project exports `AIBS::Core`, `AIBS::World`, `AIBS::Authoring` and aggregate `AIBS::Engine` through an installed CMake package. Debug and Release tests include a separate consumer configured against the installed SDK. Whole-world copies, numeric attributes and in-memory operation history are an initial foundation subset; the broader module diagram below remains the target. The native AI-BS source now offers Engineering & Specialized Labs → AI-BS Game Engine Build with worker-driven progress, recent source/design updates, build logs and C:/D:/E: free-space observations. Full creator authoring inside AI-BS remains future work; existing packaged executables have not been rebuilt. The available Google Drive connector does not expose account quota.
+
+AI-BS owns the scene/world format, runtime, renderer, SDK, editor contracts, asset pipeline and player package. Generic engine mechanisms remain reusable; First Boot-specific power/fault/story rules reside in the game module. A second mechanically different small project proves reuse.
+
+Windows x64 C++20 and DX12 are proposed initial implementation choices. The existing native Qt/Python studio is an optional editor client. The standalone player does not require Python, Qt, browser UI, Ollama, ComfyUI or running backend services for core play. Connected chat is an explicitly supported enrichment path.
+
+### 14.1a Current native graphics and deployment slice
+
+SDK 0.3.0 adds `AIBS::Platform` and `AIBS::Render`, a standalone `aibs_motherboard.exe`, offline HLSL compilation and installed shader assets. It renders actual triangle geometry for PCB traces, CPU/socket, RAM towers, slots/storage and an illuminated RTX-style landmark. Native controls are WASD/arrows, right-drag look, Space/Ctrl elevation, Shift speed and Esc/normal window close. This is a free-flight graphics preview; it is not a full campaign, CAD replica, collision simulation or persistent scene editor.
+
+Actual hardware verification: RTX 4090, DX12 feature level 12.2, 15 frames, three resize/recreate cycles, minimize/restore suspension, explicit GPU readback 960×640 and 317,660 non-background pixels. Windows Graphics Tools was installed successfully without reboot; Debug and Release runs then exercised the D3D12 debug layer with zero reported errors and warnings. The packaged renderer was also executed and verified from the deployed Studio assets. Driver version observed: 32.0.16.2101. This does not establish all-driver/device-removal/long-run guarantees or GPU-based validation.
+
+Scoped platform decision: the candidate plan used SDL3. The first native proof uses the existing Windows SDK's Win32 window/input APIs behind pimpl interfaces, avoiding an unpinned external package. This is an interim implemented Windows adapter, not a silent cancellation of SDL3 or cross-platform design. Sequential-flip double buffering preserves completed buffer contents for GPU capture; fences/resource barriers coordinate per-buffer reuse, resize and teardown. DXC 1.8.2502.11 compiles shader-model-6.0 blobs offline. Checkpoint format 1 is unchanged. [Render slice details](../../engine/RENDER_SLICE.md).
+
+The renderer's generated scene is currently separate from CreatorSession/persistent world extraction. Next integration: map stable scene entities into visible objects and allow a validated edit/save/reload to change the rendered scene. Current graphics success does not prove that broader integration exists.
+
+Deployment order honored: the existing Firebase `ai-bs-dashboard` site was published first, with `/?tab=bs_game` under normal workspace login and a public `/bs-game/index.html` playground. The site preserves other tools, including the Unreal bridge. The final published snapshot includes the actual native GPU frame and SDK 0.3 milestone status. Hosted file readback and real Chromium interaction tests passed; private memoir, ledger, photos, saves, keys and local build logs were not added to public hosting.
+
+Native Studio v5.329.87 was freshly frozen/built, verified in a real packaged Qt game-workspace smoke, and five existing Desktop/Start Menu shortcuts were updated with original .lnk bytes retained. Older releases are preserved. The package includes the HTML/local pinned Three.js assets and the C++ renderer/shaders. The game workspace launches the native preview or optional external-browser concept and displays local build, GPU, checkpoint, hosting and storage evidence. Existing host services remain separate; this is not a backend/cloud model deployment. [Deployment record](../../engine/DEPLOYMENT.md).
+
+Browser usability fix: right-drag/arrow-key look, camera-relative WASD, wheel zoom, visible target hints and E/left-click raycast interactions. A nearby core repair visibly energizes traces/GPU and updates Adam's acknowledgement only after the concept action succeeds. Garage, gate, archive, RAM and GPU targets provide additional concept interactions. Catalog modals pause movement; focus/blur clear held inputs. Hosted and packaged-browser smoke tests exercise these actual inputs. Browser state remains simulated/reset-on-reload and cannot access native saves. The new supplied HTML fragment is kept as provenance/reference, not treated as a complete new game or engineering instruction.
