+
+### 19.3 Cross-system acceptance scenarios
+
+1. Start in Jenison, obtain a tool/skimmer, choose a path, establish a claim and save/reopen the changed world.
+2. Build a bridge: player crossing works; NPC/fleet routing updates after ready navigation; removal invalidates current access while preserving historical crossings.
+3. Open/close doors and build/dismantle machines: dependent quests and hints remain coherent, with alternate progression.
+4. Liberate, reinforce and lose a sector: production, routes, agents, structures and recovery reflect actual events rather than a reset capture flag.
+5. Defeat BS: respawn while world losses persist; recover eligible carried resources without duplication.
+6. Generate a new world's story from established canon; resolve an event; reload and retain its actual outcome.
+7. Destroy a backup/capture an escaped agent: subsequent generation respects that lost source of recurring influence.
+8. Build a vehicle, overload/rebalance/reconfigure cooling, retest, wreck and recover it; retain configuration, damage and cargo correctly.
+9. Pause/close the game: war time stops; real chat does not silently advance it.
+10. Connect a companion remotely, use game tools, change session/save, disconnect: reject stale commands and preserve context boundaries.
+11. Delay/disable inference: movement, combat, native Adam continuity, authored guidance and saves remain usable.
+12. Save fails: preserve the last committed checkpoint and report failure; do not claim applied changes reached disk.
+13. Create and package a second small game through AI, visuals and code without adding its story rules to generic engine source.
+
+All are future acceptance requirements. Documentation checks or concept-image inspection do not satisfy them.
